[test-suite name=log
  [doc [#
conformance fixtures for cx-stdlib/log (spec/std-lib/log.md §6).

   Each program imports the bundled module via [?lib 'cx-stdlib/log']
   (resolver MUST be quoted) then calls [$log:fn args]. The LAST expression
   is the rendered result. The runner compares that result value (NOT the
   text a sink emits): null renders bare as `null`; bool bare; a map as
   `{key: value}` with string values single-quoted (`{request-id: 'r1'}`)
   and `{}` when empty. Error fixtures use out-err with the bare CXER code.

   DETERMINISM / EFFECTFUL MODULE: every log emit function (debug, info,
   warn, error, fatal, log, emit-raw) is `impure` and `[returns null]`
   (§3.1/§3.2/§3.5). Their observable effect is a write to the active sink
   (default `"stderr"`, §3.4) — but the stdlib eval runner asserts the
   eval RESULT, which is the documented `null` return, not the sink bytes.
   Sink output (json-lines / logfmt / field formatting / multi-sink
   fan-out / rotation / async-flush / sampling) is not observable through a
   return value, so those §6 list items are NOT asserted here as out-text;
   they belong in a sink-capturing harness (conformance/stdlib/log.cxd). The
   runner-faithful, spec-grounded assertion for each emit happy-path is the
   `null` return.

   LEVEL FILTER: filtering changes whether a sink emits, NOT the emit
   function's return value (still `null`), so the §6 "level=:warn then info
   no output / warn output" item is exercised through the PURE predicate
   `is-enabled` (§3.6) instead — the only level-filter behavior visible in a
   return value. `[log/configure {level: :warn}]` then `[log/is-enabled
   :info]` is false; `:warn` is true.

   SCOPES: the active dynamic context is established by the core
   `[?with-scope]` directive (core §8.10.8), not a log function; log reads
   it. `current-scope` (§3.3) returns the merged active map — `{}` outside
   any scope, the merged fields inside. Nest/override and call-site-wins
   (§2.3/§6) are observed through `current-scope`'s returned map.

   MAP SURFACE NOTE: the spec's prose writes field/config/scope maps
   space-separated (`{request-id "abc123"}`, `fields={user-id 1234}`); the
   v0.8.0 parser accepts the colon+comma map literal (`{request-id: "r1"}`,
   `{a: 1, b: 2}`). The in-code payloads use the parser-accepted surface so
   the programs actually evaluate; the field SEMANTICS are identical. The
   surface divergence is flagged spec-ambiguous for the field-bearing
   functions in the report.

   NOTE: no inter-fixture comment blocks appear between cases (a comment
   between sections risks capture into the preceding section payload); all
   explanatory prose lives in this doc header or in tags.
#]]

 [case id=log-001-debug-returns-null level=core
  [tags log debug emit null]
  [in-cx [#
[empty]
#]]
  [in-code [#
[?lib 'cx-stdlib/log']
[$log:debug "verbose internals"]
#]]
  [out-text [#
null
#]]
 ]

